  "checklist": [
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "RP2040 needs two supplies: 3.3 V for the I/O and 1.1 V for the core. An internal regulator makes the 1.1 V from the 3.3 V, so you only supply 3.3 V. This block takes 3.3 V from outside; the reference design makes it from USB 5 V with an NCP1117 3.3 V regulator (1 A) that has a 10 uF capacitor on its input and output.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 6,
        "where": "Sec. 2.1; input supply p. 7"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "Decouple with a 100 nF capacitor on each power pin, close to the pins. The reference design makes one exception: pins 48 and 49 share one capacitor, which saves routing space at the cost of some decoupling.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 7,
        "where": "Sec. 2.1.2; exception p. 8"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "Connect VREG_VOUT to the DVDD pins, and fit 1 uF close to both VREG_VIN and VREG_VOUT. The regulator limits capacitor ESR; small ceramic chip capacitors meet it.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 8,
        "where": "Sec. 2.1.3"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "Use a quad SPI flash and wire the QSPI pins to it directly with short connections. The W25Q128JVS (128 Mbit) is the largest size RP2040 supports; a smaller, cheaper part is fine if you need less space.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 9,
        "where": "Sec. 2.2"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "QSPI_SS is a boot strap. If it is low at reset, RP2040 starts in BOOTSEL mode and appears as a USB drive. Keep the 1 kOhm series resistor between QSPI_SS and the BOOTSEL header so the pin can still drive against the pull-down while booting. The flash wants its chip select at its supply voltage during power-up: the reference design has a 10 kOhm pull-up footprint for that, not fitted for this flash.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 9,
        "where": "Sec. 2.2, Figure 7"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "Use the 12 MHz crystal circuit as drawn: 15 pF capacitors to ground on each side and a 1 kOhm series resistor on XOUT. It is tuned for the Abracon ABM8-272-T3 (ESR 50 ohm max, 10 pF load capacitance) at IOVDD = 3.3 V. A different crystal, or another I/O voltage, needs testing over temperature.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 10,
        "where": "Sec. 2.3 and 2.3.1, Table 1"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "Put 27 ohm series resistors on USB_DP and USB_DM, close to the chip. The pins have their own pull-ups and pull-downs, so no external ones are needed. Aim for 90 ohm differential impedance: on a 1 mm board that is about 0.8 mm tracks with a 0.15 mm gap, over solid ground. A thicker board changes this.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 11,
        "where": "Sec. 2.4.1"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "RP2040 comes in a 56-pin 7 x 7 mm QFN with 0.4 mm pitch, which is hard to hand-solder. The reference design is two layers with all parts on the top side.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 5,
        "where": "Sec. 2"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "recommended",
      "text": "The reset button from RUN to ground and the SWD header are additions to the reference design, which brings RUN, SWCLK and SWD out to its I/O header. Toggling RUN restarts the chip.",
      "basis": "convention",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 9,
        "where": "Sec. 2.2"
      }
    }
  ]
